To perform a Fourier transform on a given signal in MATLAB, you can use the `fft` function. Here's an example code snippet:
```
% Generate a sample signal
Fs = 1000; % Sampling frequency (Hz)
t = 0:1/Fs:1-1/Fs; % Time vector (s)
f = 10; % Signal frequency (Hz)
x = sin(2*pi*f*t); % Signal
% Perform Fourier transform
N = length(x);
X = fft(x);
X_mag = abs(X/N); % Magnitude spectrum
f = (0:N-1)*(Fs/N); % Frequency vector (Hz)
% Plot frequency spectrum
plot(f, X_mag);
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)');
ylabel('Magnitude');
```
This code generates a sample sinusoidal signal with a frequency of 10 Hz and performs a Fourier transform on it. The resulting frequency spectrum is plotted.
To ensure that the frequency resolution is within a certain range, you can adjust the sampling frequency `Fs` and the length of the signal `N`. The frequency resolution is given by `Fs/N`, so increasing `Fs` or decreasing `N` will increase the frequency resolution.
To analyze the frequency content of an audio signal, you can read in the audio file using the `audioread` function and then perform a Fourier transform on it using the code above. However, audio signals often contain noise and distortion, which can affect the accuracy of the frequency spectrum. To account for this, you can apply a window function to the signal before performing the Fourier transform. This can help reduce spectral leakage and improve the accuracy of the frequency spectrum. Here's an example code snippet:
```
% Read in audio file
[x, Fs] = audioread('example_audio.wav');
% Apply window function
win = hann(length(x));
x_win = x.*win;
% Perform Fourier transform
N = length(x_win);
X = fft(x_win);
X_mag = abs(X/N); % Magnitude spectrum
f = (0:N-1)*(Fs/N); % Frequency vector (Hz)
% Plot frequency spectrum
plot(f, X_mag);
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)');
ylabel('Magnitude');
```
In this code, the Hann window function is applied to the audio signal before performing the Fourier transform. This can help reduce the effects of any noise or distortion present in the signal. The resulting frequency spectrum is plotted.

Certainly! Here is a JavaScript implementation of the merge sort algorithm that sorts the list of integers you provided:
```
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const middle = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, middle);
  const right = arr.slice(middle);

  return merge(mergeSort(left), mergeSort(right));
}
function merge(left, right) {
  let result = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] < right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i]);
      i++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[j]);
      j++;
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
const unsortedArr = [10, 7, 4, 8, 13, 6];
const sortedArr = mergeSort(unsortedArr);
console.log(sortedArr);
```
The `mergeSort` function takes an array as input and recursively divides it into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element. Then, the `merge` function is called to merge the subarrays back together in sorted order.
To print the sorted list in ascending order, you can simply loop through the `sortedArr` array and print each element:
```
for (let i = 0; i < sortedArr.length; i++) {
  console.log(sortedArr[i]);
}
``` 
This will print the integers in ascending order, from smallest to largest.

