Can you explain the phenomenon behind the blue color of the sky with the help of HTML code?
The blue color of the sky is caused by the scattering of sunlight by the atmosphere. Short-wavelength light, such as blue and violet light, is scattered more than long-wavelength light, such as red and yellow light. This causes the blue light to be scattered in all directions and makes the sky appear blue.
To simulate this phenomenon in HTML, we can create a gradient background that goes from a lighter blue color at the top to a darker blue color at the bottom. We can also add clouds and sun rays to make the scene more realistic. Here is an example HTML code for this:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Blue Sky</title>
    <style>
      body {
        background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #9cd5f5 0%, #0072c6 100%);
        background-repeat: no-repeat;
        height: 100%;
      }

      .cloud {
        background: #fff;
        border-radius: 50%;
        position: absolute;
      }

      .sun {
        background: #fdd835;
        border-radius: 50%;
        position: absolute;
      }

      .sun-rays {
        border-radius: 50%;
        height: 100%;
        position: absolute;
        width: 100%;
      }

      .sun-rays::before {
        background: #fdd835;
        content: "";
        height: 60%;
        left: 20%;
        position: absolute;
        top: 20%;
        transform: rotate(45deg);
        width: 60%;
      }

      .sun-rays::after {
        background: #fdd835;
        content: "";
        height: 60%;
        left: 20%;
        position: absolute;
        top: 20%;
        transform: rotate(-45deg);
        width: 60%;
      }

      .cloud-1 {
        height: 50px;
        width: 100px;
        top: 20%;
        left: 30%;
      }

      .cloud-2 {
        height: 70px;
        width: 130px;
        top: 30%;
        left: 50%;
      }

