Here's a Java function that modifies the input array in-place to convert all elements into non-negative integers without altering their relative order:
```java
public static void nonNegative(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < 0) {
            arr[i] = 0;
        }
    }
}
```
The time complexity of this solution is O(n) since we only need to iterate through the array once. The space complexity is O(1) since we are modifying the input array in-place and not using any extra memory.
To maintain the original order while moving negative elements to the end of the array, we can use a two-pointer approach where one pointer (i) scans the array from left to right and another pointer (j) keeps track of the index of the next negative element. Whenever we encounter a positive element, we swap it with the next negative element (if any) and increment j. Here's the Java function that implements this approach:
```java
public static void nonNegativeAndOrder(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    int j = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < 0) {
            continue;
        }
        if (i != j) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[j];
            arr[j] = temp;
        }
        j++;
    }
}
```
The time complexity of this solution is still O(n) since we only need to iterate through the array once. The space complexity is O(1) since we are modifying the input array in-place and not using any extra memory.
To further optimize these solutions, we can use bitwise operations instead of arithmetic operations to check for negative numbers and swap elements. This can improve the performance of the function since bitwise operations are faster than arithmetic operations.

