How can we implement a binary search algorithm in Java to search for an element in a sorted array? Can you provide an example of how to handle edge cases and avoid common pitfalls?
Here's an example of implementing a binary search algorithm in Java:
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int x) {
   int left = 0;
   int right = arr.length - 1;
   while (left <= right) {
      int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
      if (arr[mid] == x) {
         return mid;
      } else if (arr[mid] < x) {
         left = mid + 1;
      } else {
         right = mid - 1;
      }
   }
   return -1;
}
To handle edge cases and avoid common pitfalls, we should make sure that the array is sorted in ascending order before performing the binary search. We should also check for the case where the element is not present in the array by returning -1. Additionally, we should avoid integer overflow by using the formula "left + (right - left) / 2" to calculate the midpoint.

