One example of a machine learning algorithm that heavily uses probabilistic reasoning in its computations is the Naive Bayes algorithm. Naive Bayes is a classification algorithm that is based on Bayes' theorem. It assumes that the presence or absence of a particular feature is independent of the presence or absence of any other feature. This is why it is called "naive." 
The algorithm calculates the probability of a given feature belonging to a particular class based on the probabilities of the features in that class. It then selects the class with the highest probability as the classification for that feature.
The reliance on probability in the Naive Bayes algorithm can affect both the accuracy and efficiency of the algorithm. Some advantages of using probability include:
1. It can handle a large number of features and classes efficiently.
2. It can handle missing data and noisy data.
3. It can be trained on small amounts of data.
However, there are also some disadvantages to using probability:
1. It assumes that the features are independent, which may not be true in some cases.
2. If there are rare events in the data, the algorithm may not perform well.
3. It can be sensitive to the choice of prior probabilities.
